From 17a2d4f3da3e7d13f2562f2e18a193d382daa76d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 15:29:16 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#117): follow the tile's identity into child render
 passes, and never weave a region the page still owns
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The third and last mechanism behind #117's raw-SBS symptom, reported from
hardware against the 0080 build: 0080's two defects are fixed (the duplicate-id
guard is silent and id-miss is ~0 in steady state), but a tile crossing the TOP
of the viewport still renders raw side-by-side — and, worse, the gallery's own
sticky header title came back INTERLACED above it. Unlike the first two defects
this one is positional in a specific way: it reproduces only where the tile
slides under the frosted header, and the bottom sweep weaves correctly.

The live marker names the shape exactly, every frame:

  ID-MATCH FALLBACK: reason=layer_id stamped but no root quad carries it
    id=..0007 layer_id=92 target=60,218 1900x198 among 23 root quads
  NO match for target=60,218 1900x198 among 23 root quads
  plane-split: tiles=3 … tiles_without_k=1 excluded{… rpdq=1 …} flat{… frosted=1 …}

MECHANISM — THE QUAD IS NOT IN THE ROOT PASS.

A CSS backdrop-filter cannot be evaluated without a defined backdrop, so Blink
forces a render surface onto the effect node ENCLOSING the filtered element:

  third_party/blink/renderer/platform/graphics/compositing/property_tree_manager.cc
    PropertyTreeManager::SynthesizeCcEffectsForClipsIfNeeded()
      // An effect node can't omit render surface if it has child with backdrop
      // effect, in order to define the scope of the backdrop.
      effect_tree_.MutableNode(current_.effect_id).render_surface_reason =
          cc::RenderSurfaceReason::kBackdropScope;

kBackdropScope is NOT in IsConditionalRenderSurfaceReason() (that list is
kBlendModeDstIn / kOpacity / kOpacityAnimation only), so unlike an opacity
surface it is never dropped again for controlling too few layers: once a
backdrop-filter is in play, the enclosing scope ALWAYS materialises. Every
layer inside that scope — including an inline-3D canvas's ForeignLayer —
therefore draws into a CHILD aggregated pass and reaches the root only through
a RenderPassDrawQuad.

Both join sites searched root-pass quads and nothing else:

  * SurfaceAggregator::CopyQuadsToPass recorded a candidate only under
    `if (resolved_pass.is_root() && …)`;
  * DirectRenderer::ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit built its front-to-back candidate
    list from `root->quad_list`.

So the identity join was STRUCTURALLY unable to find this tile — no scoring
improvement could ever have reached it, which is why 0080 fixed the two defects
it did and left this one standing. No match → no canvas resource → no
suppression → the page drew its raw SBS buffer, and the draw-time split
reported the same fact from the other end as tiles_without_k=1.

Which effect node becomes the scope is page-dependent (it is whichever cc
effect is open when the frosted chunk is emitted), which is exactly why one
tile of three lost its join while the other two kept theirs and kept weaving.
This patch therefore does not try to PREDICT the isolation; it follows the
identity wherever it went and reports where that was.

MECHANISM 3b — AND THE HEADER WAS WOVEN.

The interlaced title is a second, independent defect that the same scroll
position exposes, and it is the more serious half because it corrupts content
that has nothing to do with inline-3D. Display::DrawAndSwap submitted a weave
rect for EVERY tracked rect, unconditionally, including tiles it had just
failed to resolve and therefore had NOT suppressed from the page raster. Those
rects still hold ordinary page pixels; for a tile straddling the top of the
viewport, clipped to a 198px strip, those pixels are the site's sticky header.
The runtime was told to weave it, and did.

That is the exact inverse of web#12's "never weave the hole" law, and it slips
past that law's own guard: the GPU-side check refuses to SNAPSHOT such a tile,
but 0074's keep-previous rescue then re-submits the rect from inside that very
branch, so the rect reached the runtime's batch anyway.

FIX, IN THREE PARTS.

(1) THE JOIN FOLLOWS THE IDENTITY (surface_aggregator).

root_resource_quads_ becomes resource_quads_ — the old name was load-bearing
documentation and is now false — and records from every dest pass, each entry
tagged with its AggregatedRenderPassId. The root-space mapping is unchanged and
already correct for a child pass: transform_to_root_target is defined for every
pass, not just root ones. Both joins gain the same admissibility filter and a
root-pass preference on ties. Non-root recording is capped (1024) so a
pathological page degrades a child-pass tile to its pre-#117 behaviour rather
than to an unbounded per-frame allocation; the root recording is uncapped, as
before.

WHICH PASSES MAY OWN WOVEN PIXELS. Matching a quad is a promise: the resource
joins the suppression set, its quad leaves the page raster, and the weave
draw-back — one whole-window kSrcOver blit in ROOT space — is what puts pixels
back. That trade is sound only if the pass reaches the root through
RenderPassDrawQuads that do nothing a root-space blit cannot reproduce. So a
pass is WEAVABLE iff it is the root, or it is drawn by exactly one trivially
composited RPDQ (opacity 1, kSrcOver, no filters / backdrop-filters / mask, no
sorting context, 2d-axis-aligned) from a weavable parent. Refusing HERE rather
than downstream is deliberate: this is the one place that sees the whole pass
list, and a refusal propagates by itself — no match, so no canvas resource, so
no suppression, so no weave rect, so no k at draw time. Every stage agrees
without a second channel. A pass drawn twice is refused outright.

The residual is named, not hidden: a tile inside a non-trivially composited
group (an opacity or filter ancestor) still draws raw SBS, as it did before,
and the fallback marker now says so in its own words rather than blaming a
child pass. Fixing THAT needs a mono draw primitive in the renderer, not a
better join. The frosted-header case is trivial by construction —
kBackdropScope is a plain isolation — which is why it is the case that gets
fixed.

(2) WEAVE SUBMISSION MOVES IN LOCKSTEP WITH PAGE PREPARATION (display).

A rect is submitted only if this frame prepared it: the tile has a resolved
canvas layer, OR nothing resolved at all this frame (in which case no canvas
quad was suppressed and snapshotting the page is the legitimate pre-#18 weave
source). That is precisely the predicate the GPU stage already applies one
stage later; moving it EARLIER is what stops the rect reaching the runtime's
batch, which the GPU-side skip could not do on its own. The tile loses nothing
it had — unsuppressed, the page draws it exactly as a page with no inline-3D
would. Withholding is counted at LOG(ERROR) on 0073's discipline; it is the
correct outcome but also the moment a tile stops weaving, so it may not be
silent.

(3) THE DRAW-TIME SPLIT FOLLOWS THE TILE, AND D-PRIME GENERALISES
    (direct_renderer).

ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit now takes the whole draw-order list and collects
candidates by DESCENT from the root — identity accumulated transform there, so
the root pass's own numbers are bit-identical to what it produced before, and
every child expressed relative to exactly that convention rather than to a
second one read out of transform_to_root_target. It descends only into passes
that touch a tile, and is capped at 32 passes / 8192 quads.

A tile whose home pass is not the root gets NO over-plane: lifting a quad out
of a child pass changes what it composites against, the same reason the v1
exclusions refuse a non-kSrcOver blend. What it gets instead is D-prime
(browser#88), generalised. D-prime already knows how to leave a region to the
page — clip the woven draw-back out of it and put the same rect in the flat
list so a capable panel switches its 3D element off there — and that is exactly
the treatment every quad in front of the tile needs. Both the tile's own pass
and the ANCESTOR SPINE are walked, since a quad drawn after this pass's RPDQ in
the parent is in front of the tile just as surely as one after k here; walking
the spine is also what makes the fix independent of which effect node became
the surface.

WHY THIS KEEPS THE D-PRIME CONTRACT. D-prime says the frost samples PRE-weave
page pixels and reads flat behind the glass. Under this patch the frost band is
withheld from the draw-back, so the page's own frosted glass survives crisp,
and what it blurred is the page behind the suppressed canvas — a flat patch of
the scene, which is D-prime's stated and accepted loss, not a new one. It is
not a hole: the canvas quad's suppression exposes the page background still
drawn beneath it. So the tile reads woven 3D below the header band and non-SBS
2D within it, with the header itself untouched — the two unacceptable outcomes,
raw SBS anywhere and a black band, are both excluded by construction.

Withheld rects are exact — never merged, never truncated (under-punching weaves
over 2D; a bounding box punches pixels the tile should have woven) — deduped
losslessly by containment, and BOUNDED: each is one kDifference clip on the
draw-back's single blit, so over 64 per tile the tile withholds its whole rect
for that frame. That reads as a flat card, which is a degradation and not a
corruption.

INSTRUMENTATION, so the next hardware run NAMES the mechanism instead of
inferring it: an "inline-3D CHILD-PASS join" marker carrying the pass id (a
non-root id here IS the backdrop-scope isolation, observed rather than
assumed), the fallback marker split into "no quad anywhere" vs "found but
inadmissible", and tiles_in_child_pass / tiles_withheld_whole / passes /
passes_over_cap on the plane-split line.

